course: dictionary
lesson: 6363
title: Ms, a title and an abbreviation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ms is a title you put before a woman's surname, as in «Ms Patel». You say it «miz», and it begins with a capital M.
explain: At many English schools, pupils call a teacher by a title and surname, as in «Good morning, Ms Brown.» Until an adult asks you to use a first name, use Mr, Mrs, Ms or Miss with the surname.
explain: Small letters, «ms», mean something else: millisecond, one thousandth of a second. A fast reaction takes about 200 ms. The context and the capital letter tell you which meaning is right.
fact: Ms is a title used before a woman's surname, whether she is married or not.
fact: Say it «miz». Write it with a capital M, as Ms (or Ms. in US English).
fact: «ms» in small letters means millisecond, one thousandth of a second.
fact: MS can also stand for manuscript or for multiple sclerosis, an illness.
fact: You can use Mr, Mrs, Ms or Miss with a surname for teachers and other adults.
exercise: «Good morning, Ms Brown.» What is Ms, in this sentence? | A kind of school bell | A sound a door makes | A first name for a girl | *A title used before a woman's surname
exercise: A reaction takes about 200 ms. What does ms mean here? | *Millisecond, one thousandth of a second | Minutes | Mister Smith | Manuscript
exercise: Which of these is a title for a woman, not a man? | Mr | *Ms | Sir | Dr
test: «After hearing the story, one girl admitted she had signed a note herself, and Ms Rivera helped her tell her mum.» Who is Ms Rivera? | Her mum | A bus driver | A boy in the class | *A woman, called by her surname
test: In a science lesson, «ms» stands for what? | A minute | *A millisecond | Mister | A manuscript
exam: A girl writes a note: «Dear Ms Smith». What does Ms show? | A man's title | *A polite title for a woman, married or not | A short form of millisecond | A kind of school bell
